A tuple needs to be fixed-length in order for you to establish that it's definitely non-empty. I think a more robust way to check this would be

```suggestion
        // Tuple inference preserves individual names, including names supplied indirectly:
        //
        //     names = ("first", "second")
        //     __slots__ = names
        //
        // An unknown tuple element prevents recovering every name, but still proves the
        // declaration is nonempty.
        if let Some(tuple) = slots_ty.tuple_instance_spec(db, &env)
            && tuple.len().minimum() > 1
        {
            let Tuple::Fixed(tuple) = &*tuple else {
                return SlotDefinition::NonEmpty;
            };

            return tuple
                .iter_all_elements()
                .map(|element| {
                    element
                        .as_string_literal()
                        .map(|literal| Name::new(literal.value(db)))
                })
                .collect::<Option<Box<[_]>>>()
                .map_or(SlotDefinition::NonEmpty, SlotDefinition::Names);
        }
```

this would allow you to infer that classes like this (which come up all the time, obviously) definitely have non-empty `__slots__`, even if we don't know all the names in the `__slots__` definition:

```py
def test(x: tuple[str, *tuple[str, ...]]):
    class Foo:
        __slots__ = x
```

